@ilyam8 and @stelfrag I agree with both of you and netdata will not connect more when there is a `/` on  the string.
I am keeping the message inside the socket library, because the function is called from other parts of the code and the error could happen there.
@amoss I understood your point of view and case we parse, we could do the things more clean for the users, but the function where we are adding messages does not force the people to specify the protocol and port, so my suggestion is to keep the message as it is, for the user compare his current input with the format expected. I am open to continue talking about this.

I wondered about handling `tcp://` directly as well. The `//` is not legal syntax for any kind of host that we specify. But let's not kick the can down the road indefinitely:

* `connect_to_this` is called from exactly one place in the entire repo (`connect_to_one_of`).
* `connect_to_one_of` is called from exactly two places in the entire repo, the one that we are investigating and `./backends/backends.c:997`.

So the only places that a change could have an impact is here, and in the backend config (or the new exporters config that is replacing it). Tolerating 'tcp://' is backward-compatible -> there are no legal configurations that it could impact.

So a better fix all-around is to remove the new warning message and change the `connect_to_this` code to this:
```
    if(strncmp(host, "tcp:", 4) == 0) {
        host += 4;
        protocol = IPPROTO_TCP;
        socktype = SOCK_STREAM;
        if(host[0]=='/' && host[1]=='/')
           host += 2;
    }
```

Then update the string in the docs to match and fix the comment so that it matches the documentation and the functionality in the code.

Thoughts?

